<!-- WHY VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Why Visit Kashgar</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#129511;</div><h3>Legendary Bazaar</h3><p>Experience the Kashgar Sunday Market — one of Central Asia's largest and most authentic bazaars. Thousands of merchants trade everything from carpets and spices to livestock and silk, just as they have for millennia.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#128332;</div><h3>Living History</h3><p>Lose yourself in Kashgar's Old City, a maze of mud-brick houses, narrow alleyways, and ornate Uyghur architecture. Artisans still practice traditional crafts — copper-working, pottery, instrument-making — in their homes.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#128747;</div><h3>Karakoram Highway</h3><p>Drive one of the world's highest paved roads along the ancient trade route to Pakistan. The journey to Karakul Lake passes through dramatic mountain scenery with views of Muztagh Ata (7,546m) and the Pamir Plateau.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127757;</div><h3>Cultural Crossroads</h3><p>Kashgar has been the meeting point of Chinese, Central Asian, Persian, and Turkic cultures for over 2,000 years. Few places on earth offer such a rich tapestry of traditions, languages, and cuisines in one city.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TOP ATTRACTIONS -->
<section class="section section-alt"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Top Attractions</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="tours-grid">
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/xinjiang-landscape.jpg" alt="Kashgar Sunday Market" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#1</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Kashgar Sunday Market</h3><p>The beating heart of Kashgar and a photographer's dream. Arrive early to watch the livestock section come alive with donkeys, sheep, and camels being traded. Then explore the sprawling goods market for carpets, hats, knives, spices, and silk — bargaining is expected and part of the fun.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Id Kah Mosque" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#2</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Id Kah Mosque</h3><p>China's largest mosque, built in 1442, anchors the city's spiritual life. Its yellow-tiled facade and minarets dominate the central square. During Eid, up to 100,000 worshippers gather here. Non-Muslims are welcome outside prayer times — dress modestly and remove shoes.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Kashgar Old City" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#3</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Kashgar Old City (Gaotai)</h3><p>The Gaotai ancient residential area features earth-built homes stacked on a loess cliff, some over 400 years old. While much has been renovated, wandering the alleyways reveals traditional courtyards, baking tandoor ovens, and kids playing in the lanes — timeless scenes of Uyghur daily life.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Abakh Khoja Tomb" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#4</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Abakh Khoja Tomb (Fragrant Concubine Tomb)</h3><p>This magnificent 17th-century mausoleum complex is the holiest Muslim site in Xinjiang. The turquoise-tiled dome, intricate geometric patterns, and tranquil gardens are a masterpiece of Uyghur-Islamic architecture. The legendary Fragrant Concubine of the Qing emperor is said to be buried here.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Karakoram Highway" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#5</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Karakoram Highway &amp; Karakul Lake</h3><p>Drive the fabled KKH, often called the Eighth Wonder of the World. The 4-hour journey from Kashgar to Karakul Lake (3,600m) passes through stunning gorges and the Kunlun Mountains. The turquoise alpine lake at the foot of Muztagh Ata is an unforgettable sight.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- BEST TIME TO VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127800;</div><h3>Spring (Apr-May)</h3><p>Mild temperatures (15-25&deg;C), blooming apricot and peach blossoms, and pleasant walking weather. The market buzzes with spring activity. Occasional dust storms in April — pack sunglasses and a scarf.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127774;</div><h3>Autumn (Sep-Oct)</h3><p>The golden season — clear blue skies, comfortable temperatures (12-25&deg;C), and the grape and melon harvest in full swing. Golden poplar leaves along the Karakoram Highway create spectacular photo opportunities. The Sunday Market is at its most vibrant.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9924;</div><h3>Summer (Jun-Aug)</h3><p>Warm days (25-35&deg;C) and cool nights. The long daylight hours are perfect for exploring. June-August is the best window for the Karakoram Highway when the pass is fully open. Pack layers — temperatures drop dramatically at altitude.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#10052;</div><h3>Winter (Nov-Mar)</h3><p>Cold (-10 to 5&deg;C) with occasional snow. The Karakoram Highway may be impassable. However, the Old City takes on a quiet, atmospheric beauty, and you'll have attractions virtually to yourself. Hotels offer significant discounts.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

